<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to optional, singular, and multiple values

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.MultiplicityAndCollections-->
<docs:MultiplicityAndCollections xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to optional, singular, and multiple values
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: optional, singular, and multiple values, multiplicity brackets, and collection
    semantics, grounded in the multiplicity sections of
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.TypeExpressions. Body content is authored in a03 from that
    source.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:TypeExpressions" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — multiplicity as a postfix type-expression operation, and nested collections.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Multiplicity and Collections</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="Multiplicity is not a separate type">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Whether a value is optional, singular, or multiple is expressed as a bracket on
a type expression, not by choosing a separate Array type. Multiplicity is one axis of how a type
expression is written, not a distinct kind of type.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Reading a multiplicity bracket">
          <content>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="A type expression with no bracket at all means exactly one value." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="An open bracket such as [] means a collection of any number of values, including none." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A bounded bracket such as [1..5] means a collection whose size must fall within that range." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Recognizing these three bracket forms is enough to read the multiplicity of any
type expression on sight.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Grouping nested collections explicitly">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Because multiplicity is a postfix operation on a type expression, a collection
of collections needs explicit grouping — the inner type expression is parenthesized before the
outer bracket is applied, rather than stacking two brackets directly. (Integer[])[] describes a
collection of Integer collections; the ungrouped form Integer[][] is not a valid way to write it.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Placing multiplicity correctly in a type expression">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A multiplicity bracket always applies to the type expression immediately
before it — a plain type name, or a parenthesized expression that already carries its own
multiplicity.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Where multiplicity is used">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Multiplicity brackets appear commonly on aggregation and association types and
on capability input types, while a plain property is usually exactly one value.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Constraints">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Constraints" summary="Learn declarative validation rules, including size bounds on a collection.">
                  <target>docs:Constraints</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:MultiplicityAndCollections>
